{"number": 163, "text": "                     Unit   28\n             28.1\n                     Pronunciation  of the sections in bold is given in brackets.\n                     A: What  are you  making?  NE   (/wotfa/)\n                     B: It’s a cake for Richard’s birthday. E\n                     A: It’s amazing, isn’t it? E (/ameizin/)\n                     B: Do  you think he’ll like it? NE (/d381nk/)\n                     A: I’m sure he will, although he’s a bit fussy about food, isn’t he? E (/fo:'/) / NE (/zabit/)\n                     B: Have  you seen this? E (/si:n/)\n                     A: Wow!   Is that a real flower? NE (/zdzt/)\n                     B: No, it’s made from sugar.  NE\n                     A: When   does it have to be ready? NE  (/wenzit/)\n                     B: It’s his birthday tomorrow. Do you know   where  he is now? NE         / NE  (/d3nau/)\n                     A: I’ve no idea. E (/nau/)\n\n             28.2\n                     (Speaker A  = Poland)\n                     12 A  ‘dibber’ is a small hand-held tool used by gardeners for making holes in soil into which\n                     seeds can be dropped.\n                     14 A  ‘brown-bag  lunch’ is a phrase mainly used in North American   English. It is food bought or\n                     prepared  at home to be eaten during a lunch break  at work, often carried in a brown paper  bag.\n                     In British English we would  be more likely to talk about a ‘packed lunch’.\n\n                     Unit   29\n             29.1\n                     1 He  wrote it.                   5 Take a  left turn.              9 We  crossed over.\n                     2 A  published article.           6 They  kepe quiet.              10    contact Ann.\n                     3 It’s in first gear.             7 It looked good.                11 He  finished first.\n                     4 It was just him.                8 We  reached Berlin.            12        badly.\